ndissimo 1999 Rio Grande Games Wolfgang Kramer\nRichard Ulrich Board El Grande EFUN (EN), EFUN-ORDER (EN) US$ 0 10.01.07  \nKönig & Intrigant Player's Edition 1998 Hans im Glück   Board El Grande EFUN-ORDER (EN), FAIRspielt (DE), ReichDerSpiele (DE) DM 0 04.11.15 Out of print\nThe El Grande Expansions   Rio Grande Games Wolfgang Kramer\nRichard Ulrich Board El Grande   US$ 24.95 16.08.07  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEl Grande: Big Box Rulebook - 1jour-1jeu.com (https://cdn.1j1ju.com/medias/15/63/b8-el-grande-big-box-rulebook.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.7 years ago; ** (This is one of the most important rules of the game!) ... The example text reads: **Example:** In a 4-player game, 1 Action card is left unused. ... If the round marker is moved to the next number, a new round begins, as described above.\nThe map shows a purple King piece in Castilla la Nueva; surrounding labeled regions include Castilla la Vieja, País Vasco, Aragón, Valencia, Sevilla, and Granada.\n</visual_element>\n\n## 4. End of round\n\nAfter all players have completed their turns, perform the following to prepare for the next round:\n\n1. Place any remaining Action cards facedown underneath the corresponding stack.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e2\">\nExample illustration of Action-card stacks: one unused Action card is shown being placed underneath its corresponding stack. The example text reads: **Example:** In a 4-player game, 1 Action card is left unused. This card is placed underneath its stack. The top card of each stack is now hidden.\n</visual_element>\n\n2. The player with the lowest Power card takes the start player marker.\n\n3. All players discard their Power card to their personal facedown Power card discard pile. Discarded Power cards can not be used again.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e3\">\nExample illustration of four Power cards and the start player marker. **Example:** *Gabriel* played the lowest Power card. He takes the start player marker. In the next round, he will be the first player to play a Power card. The other players will follow in clockwise order.\n</visual_element>\n\n**Note:** In each round, you will have one fewer Power card to choose from. Make sure to leave yourself a variety of Power cards for future rounds. (Incidentally, it is guaranteed that you will always have a Power card that you are able to play.)\n\n4. Move the round marker 1 space down.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e4\">\nRound-track illustration with two marked positions: the numbered space “2” and a fleur-de-lis space. If the round marker is moved to the next number, a new round begins, as described above. If the round marker is moved to a fleur-de-lis, general scoring occurs (see below). This will happen after the 3rd, 6th, and 9th rounds. Once general scoring is complete, move the round marker 1 space down and begin the following round.\n</visual_element>\n\nPage 5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKulkmann�s Gamebox - El Grande (https://boardgame.de/reviews/elgrande.htm)\nciteturn4search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 20.7 years ago; This figure is placed randomly at the start of the game, and it may only be moved if a player plays a \"Move King\" action-card. ... The contents are a new set of cards that replace the 2 decks in El Grande. The basic rules of El Grande stay the same, but the way cards influence the game has changed substantially.\nThe card play is what makes El Grande such a wonderful game. Low cards get large numbers of Caballeros from the Provinces to Court, but you go later in the turn. High cards get little or no Caballeros to Court, but you get first pick of the event cards (did I mention that there's 5 new event cards revealed at the start of each turn, some good, some not so good ?). At the end of every 3 turns, scoring for the most Caballeros in the regions takes place, and points are earned. Event cards can also produce bouts of scoring. The game ends after 9 turns and the winner is the player with the most points. El Grande went into my top 10 favorite games when I first played it a few months ago. It only takes just over an hour to play, and is easy enough for family and friends. Serious game players will find much to like about El Grande as well. Thoroughly recommended. Image: [IMAGE] Image: [IMAGE] Image: [IMAGE] Image: [IMAGE] I have just received the expansion kit for El Grande from Hans Im Gluck titled \"Konig & Intrigant\". The contents are a new set of cards that replace the 2 decks in El Grande. The basic rules of El Grande stay the same, but the way cards influence the game has changed substantially. Each player now holds an identical deck of 18 event cards, of which he secretly chooses 13 to play the game with. Events may now get played more than once per game, once by each player. Some new events have also been added, as well as 2 blank cards which Hans Im Gluck inform us are for a competition to invent new events for the game. Konig & Intrigant is a must buy for El Grande fans.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPotatogames | PDF | Mergers And Acquisitions | Retail (https://www.scribd.com/document/390018721/potatogames)\nciteturn4search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; designs, willingly grinding through dense rule systems to enjoy the simulation and ... Like the power cards, El Grande’s action cards represent a tradeoff, where ... The action card system in El Grande was really the harbinger of a new trend\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAsmodee MAP Holiday Sale (https://www.gamersguildusa.com/collections/asmodee-map-holiday-sale?filter.p.m.custom.mechanic=Drafting)\nciteturn4search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Players take turns depending on which power card they play. ... After 9 rounds total, somebody will be the new ruler of medieval Spain. ENGAGE IN MEDIEVAL INTRIGUE: Set in 15th century Spain, El Grande is a classic area control game where players compete to become the ruler of the land. ... CHOOSE YOUR ACTIONS: Each turn, use power cards to select from 5 action cards, allowing you to place caballeros strategically and perform special actions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding Blocks of Tabletop of Mechanisms.pdf - PDFCOFFEE.COM (https://pdfcoffee.com/building-blocks-of-tabletop-of-mechanismspdf-pdf-free.html)\nciteturn4search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.6 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; Another game that uses this mechanism is El Grande, where the playing of Power Cards determines the turn order. ... However, turn order is a much more nebulous concept for new players, and determining its value can be very tricky. ... It was pioneered in historical simulations as a way of introducing historical events without unduly adding rules overhead. For example, Twilight Struggle, a simulation of the Cold War, has a card that can either be used for 3 Action Points or the Warsaw Pact event.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://boardgamegeek.com/boardgame/93/el-grande/files\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://boardgamegeek.com/boardgame/93/el-grande/files: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHiG - Games & Expansions (https://cundco.de/en/rules/hig-games-expansions)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"pattern\":\"El Grande\"}); Total lines: 147\nL47: #### 【70†Sankt Petersburg】\nL48: \nL49: > 2026: 【71†DE Regel】  |  【72†EN Rule】\nL50: > 【73†Das Bankett】 & 【74†In bester Gesellschaft】 【75†DE Regel】\nL51: \nL52: #### 【76†Time Trouble】\nL53: \nL54: > 【77†DE R